{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Convex.SpecificFunctions.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.midpoint_concave_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.midpoint_concave_add","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LNaturalConcaveForm.lean#L210-L216","doc":"A sum of midpoint-concave pieces is midpoint concave: the property is closed under\naddition, which is why establishing it separately for the separable and the difference parts\nsuffices.  Trivial, and stated because the assembly rests on it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.neg_exp_series_concave","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.neg_exp_series_concave","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LNaturalConcaveForm.lean#L169-L208","doc":"**`C2` is a theorem, not a sample: every univariate piece of `G` is concave because it is\nMINUS a non-negative combination of decaying exponentials.**\n\nRead off the producer's own definitions, with `t = q0^2 > 1` and `L = ln t > 0`:\n\n* `epsx x = -log(1 - t^(-x)) = sum_{k>=1} (1/k) e^(-k L x)` — the Mercator series, all\n  coefficients `1/k > 0`;\n* `R x = sum_{r>=1} c_r e^(-r L x)` with `c_r = t^(-r)/(r (1 - t^(-r))) > 0`, which is exactly\n  what the code accumulates as `_ER[r] * z^r`;\n* `W y = epsx y + sum_{r>=1} ((D_r - 1)/r) e^(-r L y)`, and `D_r - 1 = sum_{delta in Delta,\n  delta >= 1} t^(-r delta) > 0` — the `- 1` is the code's own splitting off of `delta = 0`.\n\nSo all three are non-negative combinations of `e^(-r L x)`, hence convex, hence `-R(b+u)`,\n`-W(c-u)` and `-epsx(g+d)` are concave.  This lemma is that statement: the negated series is\nconcave in the two-point form `concave_exchange_inward` takes.  It is stated for a finite\n`Finset` because the producer truncates at `RSER`, and the bound is uniform in the truncation\norder — a partial sum of a non-negative combination is one too, so nothing depends on `RSER`.\n\nThat closes the hypothesis the implication consumes.  What the probe measures (52 points, zero\nviolations) is now a consistency check on this, not the evidence for it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.exp_decay_convex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.exp_decay_convex","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LNaturalConcaveForm.lean#L157-L167","doc":"**`exp(-(L x))` is convex, in the two-point form the rest of this file consumes.**  The\nengine behind `C2` below: it is `convexOn_exp` pulled back along `x ↦ -(L x)`, which is affine,\nso no derivative test and no `AffineMap` plumbing is needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.difference_between_of_parity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.difference_between_of_parity","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LNaturalConcaveForm.lean#L147-L155","doc":"**The difference-part spread, in the form the assembly uses.**  `(d_p - d_x)(d_p - d_y)` is\n`((r - e)^2 - (A - B)^2)/4`, so `parity_spread` makes it non-positive — hence `d_p` is between\n`d_x` and `d_y` and engine 1 applies to `psi`.  Stated over `ℝ` with the identity as a\nhypothesis, so the algebraic reduction is explicit rather than buried. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.between_of_product_nonpos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.between_of_product_nonpos","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LNaturalConcaveForm.lean#L130-L145","doc":"Betweenness in the product form the spread bound produces: `(d - dx) * (d - dy) ≤ 0` is\nexactly \"`d` lies between `dx` and `dy`\", which is the hypothesis engine 1 consumes once the\npair is re-parametrised.  Recorded so the two engines visibly compose. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.parity_spread","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.parity_spread","kind":"theorem","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LNaturalConcaveForm.lean#L99-L128","doc":"**Engine 2: the parity obstruction cannot occur.**  With `r, e` the parities of\n`m = x_j + y_j` and `n = x_k + y_k`, and `A = x_j - y_j`, `B = x_k - y_k`, the difference pair\n`(d_p, d_q)` lies between `(d_x, d_y)` exactly when `(r - e)^2 ≤ (A - B)^2`.  It always does:\n`A` has the parity of `m` and `B` the parity of `n`, so `r = e` makes the left side zero, and\n`r ≠ e` forces `A ≠ B` and hence `|A - B| ≥ 1`.\n\nThis is the step that rules out the one configuration that would break the difference part —\n`(d_p, d_q) = (1, -1)` against `(d_x, d_y) = (0, 0)`, which for concave `psi` would demand\n`psi 0 + psi 0 ≤ psi 1 + psi (-1)`, the inequality the wrong way round. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.exchange_sum_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.exchange_sum_eq","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LNaturalConcaveForm.lean#L93-L97","doc":"The inward pair really does preserve the sum, which is what makes it the floor/ceil pair of\nthe midpoint.  Stated separately because it is the half that pins the parametrisation to the\ndiscrete-midpoint setting rather than to an arbitrary pair of convex combinations. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.concave_exchange_inward","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LNaturalConcaveForm.concave_exchange_inward","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LNaturalConcaveForm.lean#L78-L91","doc":"**Engine 1: a concave function does not lose by an inward exchange.**  If `p` and `q` are a\nconvex-combination pair drawn from `x` and `y` with complementary weights — so `p + q = x + y`\nand both lie between `x` and `y` — then `phi x + phi y <= phi p + phi q`.\n\nConcavity is taken in explicit two-point form so the lemma needs no `ConcaveOn` instance, and\nthe inward pair is given by its weight `t` so the proof needs no division. ","signatureChunk":0}]}